es that they create  is not too large but in fact it's not so  difficult to show that  these two um perfect matchings because  they are independent  behave exactly like the cycles of a  permutation  and it's quite well known that the  expected number of cycles  in a permutation is that of log n and  this  is also concentrated with high  probability  so in the end we get after  taking two perfect matchings  log n many or avoided login many cycles  and then these cycles should be much  easier to join together  to form a hamilton cycle  we we apply the similar trick as before  we expose a neighborhood from from one  vertex of  every cycle um we expose logan many  neighbors  and then from the endpoint of a current  of the path we currently have we also  expose logan many  neighbors if we can find two adjacent  vertices which are  which are in these new neighborhoods  then we can form  a new a new path  which is illustrated in red here which  includes the cycle  and keeps most of this the path  structure  the same and if we do not we can simply  close or rotate the entire path and  retry with a new endpoint  so there's some technical details out  here how to store the paths and the  cycles in a way such that the testing  can be done into constant time  and the rotations can be done in login  time but  for this we can apply avl trees combined  with  linked lists and  if you're interested in further details  we would refer to level 10  of the paper thank you for your  attention  if you have any question feel free to  write an email  or ask during the talk
 

